        whereToFind: "Look on your invoice for a line saying power factor, kVA, reactive power, demand adjustment or similar. If there is not one, ask the site manager one question: am I billed on kW — the power my machines actually use — or on kVA, the larger amount the site pulls off the grid to deliver it. If the answer is kVA and your gear runs at a power factor of 0.95, you are paying for about 5% more power than your machines use, so put in 5%. The sum is 1 divided by 0.95, which is 1.05. If you are billed on kW, put in 0%. To work it out yourself you need your power factor. It is on the spec sheet for the machine's power supply, quoted at full load — most modern miners sit between 0.95 and 0.99 there, and get worse when the machines are throttled or idling. Divide 1 by that number: 1 divided by 0.95 is 1.053, so about 5%; 1 divided by 0.98 is 1.020, so about 2%. Better still, ask your host to read the power factor off your meter. That reading beats any spec sheet, because it is your machines in your building.",
